  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1255 PM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nWednesday through Monday:\n\nSynopsis: High pressure will move into the western Great Lakes \nduring the second half of the week, leading to pleasant and mostly \ndry weather conditions Wednesday night into Friday. The high will \nshift east by Saturday, with returning southerly flow leading to \nsteady increases in humidity through the remainder of the weekend. \nConcurrent increases in zonal upper level flow, embedded shortwaves, \nand passing surface fronts will support the next shower and storm \nchances within the returning moisture. Showers and storms could \nbegin as early as Saturday night, with additional periods of storm \npotential continuing into Monday as humidity lingers across the \nwestern Great Lakes. Windows of storm activity will be accompanied \nby lull periods, with all-day washouts not anticipated.\n\nSaturday Night through Monday: Shower and storm chances return amid \nincreasing warm/moist advection. Expecting initial periods of \nactivity to commence Saturday night through Sunday as the nose of \nthe low level jet approaches and ultimately folds over southern \nWisconsin, with a final period of storm development along a passing \ncold front on Monday. A broad overlap of sufficient shear and \ninstability makes this a period to monitor for organized/stronger \nthunderstorms, though whether these parameters come together to \nrealize any potential will depend on smaller-scale features & timing \nnot yet captured by available guidance. Precipitable water values \nwill be elevated through this period, so will also need to watch for \npockets of locally heavy rainfall. Will be monitoring trends & \nproviding additional info as this portion of the period draws \ncloser. \n\nQuigley",
